All numbers are small-endian (except lump sizes, which are PDP-endian).

Some lumps use DER format. Except where otherwise specified, object
identifiers may be absolute or relative, and if they are relative, are
relative to 2.25.196954517921581521497869385664052876310 (which is the
base OID for Super ZZ Zero). (Some fields are relative to a different OID;
these are noted explicitly where they occur.)


=== !SZ0 ===

This lump identifies the file as a Super ZZ Zero file.

This lump is always the first lump (due to starting with a exclamation
mark, since Super ZZ Zero will sort the lumps in order by name), and always
contains exactly seven bytes of data.

The first byte specifies the kind of file:
  0 = Save game
  1 = Unpublished world
  3 = Published world

The other six bytes are a ID which changes whenever the world file is
modified (although it is not globally unique, it is unlikely to be the
same as it was before when modified), in order that the game engine can
check if a save game file does not match the world file. In a save game
file, these six bytes match those in the world file.


=== *.BRD ===

The definition of a board. The parts are:
  Header
  Variable properties
  Stats
  Stat grid (only CURRENT.BRD and PREVIOUS.BRD)
  Grid other than stats

The header consists of:

* Encoding flags (2 bytes): Specifies which other fields are present.

* Board flags (2 bytes if bit8 of encoding flags is set, or 1 byte if bit8
of encoding flags is clear)

* Screen number (2 bytes)

* Exits: Two bytes per exit; the low four bits of the encoding flags
specifies which ones are present (the non-present ones will be zero)

* Width/height: If bit4 of encoding flags is set then these are two bytes
each, but if clear then they are one byte each and you must add 1 to each.

* User data (2 bytes if bit5 of encoding flags is set, zero if clear)

* Max stat number (1 byte)

Summary of encoding flags:
  0001 = East exit
  0002 = North exit
  0004 = West exit
  0008 = South exit
  0010 = Large board
  0020 = User data
  0040 = (reserved)
  0080 = (reserved)
  0100 = Long flag
  0200 = New stat format
  0400 = Layer inversion
  0800 = Variable properties
  1000 = (reserved)
  2000 = (reserved)
  4000 = (reserved)
  8000 = Save game

If bit11 of the encoding flags is set then the variable property list
follows. It starts with one byte being the number of variable properties,
and then each variable property consists of one type byte and then the
data; the length of the data is equal to the low nybble of the type byte.
The possible types are:

* 0x04 = Set scroll position; the data is two unsigned 16-bit numbers,
first the X scroll and then the Y scroll, of the board coordinates that
will be placed at the view center.

* 0x11 to 0x18 = Load a font; the data is the lump name excluding the .CHR
or .FNT suffix.

* 0x1F = Replace a single character from the loaded font. The first byte of
the data is the character code, and the rest is the raster data.

* 0x21 to 0x28 = Load a peltte; the data is the lump name excluding the
.PAL suffix.

Each stat (the first stat number is stat 1) is as follows:

* Flag (8-bits; only present if bit9 of encoding flags is set)

* Misc1, Misc2, Misc3 (16-bits each)

* Length of text (16-bits) (zero if no text is present)

* Text of this stat

* Speed (8-bits)

* Frame offset (16-bits)

* Mode bits (8-bits)

* Number of XY records for this stat (16-bits)

* The data of the XY records

The stat flag specifies which other fields are present, as follows:
  bit0 = Text
  bit1 = Misc1
  bit2 = Misc2
  bit3 = Misc3
  bit4 = Frame offset
  bit5 = Additional XY data
  bit6 = Mode
  bit7 = (reserved)

For each XY record, the first byte is the encoding flag, and then the rest
of the data is according to the encoding flag:
  bit1-bit0 = X coordinate
  bit3-bit2 = Y coordinate
  bit5-bit4 = Instruction pointer
  bit6 = Layer
  bit7 = Delay

* X and Y coordinates: If the encoding flag bits is 3, then a 8-bit or
16-bit number (depending on board width/height; however, the coordinates
are always 16-bits long in CURRENT.BRD). Otherwise, it is the encoding
flag bits number plus the coordinate of the previous XY record minus 1.

* Instruction pointer: Encoding flag bits 0 means 0, 1 means 65535, 2 means
same as previous, and 3 means a specified 16-bit number.

* Layer: Set means read a 8-bit number, clear means same as previous.

* Delay: Set means read a 8-bit number, clear means same as previous.

If the "additional XY data" is present, then another encoding flag follows:
  bit0 = Sensor kind
  bit1 = Sensor color
  bit2 = Sensor parameter
  bit3 = Sensor stat
  bit4 = Frame pointer
  bit7-bit5 = (reserved)

The sensor data is 8-bits each, and the frame pointer is 16-bits. The frame
pointer cannot be nonzero if the frame offset for the stat is zero, and it
also cannot exceed the length of the stat's text minus six.

The board grid is encoded in the order:
  Under/kind
  Main/kind
  Over/kind
  Under/color
  Main/color
  Over/color
  Under/parameter
  Main/parameter
  Over/parameter

It is encoded as a sequences of runs; a run is allowed to cross between
layers, but cannot cross between kind/color/parameter.

A run command in the board grid has the high 2-bits specifying where the
data comes from, and then the low 6-bits being the run length; if the
low 6-bits are zero then instead the run length is the next byte plus 127,
but if that next byte is 128 or higher, then also add 128 times the next
byte, and then add 63 more. The possible data sources are:

* 0 = A guess is made. The logic for making guesses is explained below.

* 1 = It is given explicitly, after the run length; one byte per cell.

* 2 = Each cell repeats the value of the previous cell.

* 3 = Each cell repeats the value of the cell whose distance behind this
one is equal to the board width (i.e. a "copy-above" run).

Guesses are made according to the following logic:

* For the kind, the guess is always zero if there is no stat at that
location, or the most recent kind of tile that does have a stat, if this
one does have a stat.

* For the colour and parameter, it guesses the colour or parameter of the
most recent cell of the same kind, or zero if there isn't any.

Bit11 of the encoding flags means that "layer inversion" is in use. After
decoding the board data, any cell where the kind in both the under layer
and main layer are nonzero, will have the two layers exchanged. Layer
inversion also affects stats: If they are exchanged in this way and, one
layer has a stat at those coordinates but the other doesn't, then the
layer field of the corresponding stat XY record is updated.


=== *.CHR ===

A font with 256 characters. This is stored as the raw raster data, starting
with the top scanline of character 0 and ending with the bottom scanline of
character 255. The high bit of each byte corresponds to the left pixel.

This lump must be exactly 3584 bytes long, otherwise it is an error.


=== *.HLP ===

A help file.

This has the format of the script file but the set of commands is limited:
  @ : $ ! !-
(normal text is also allowed)

Any ! command can have, if not a label name in this help file and not the
name of another help file, a command number which is the return value. This
may be either decimal, or hexadecimal with $ at front.

Line breaks are LF only. The text is not compressed.

(Support for # commands in help file is not currently implemented; if it
is implemented in future, only a few commands are likely to work.)


=== *.LIB ===

A script library. This has the same format of the script file.


=== *.OVM ===

Overlay memory. Has the same format as the MEMORY lump, but the load
address is not necessarily zero.


=== *.PAL ===

Stores a palette. The format of this lump depends on the lump size:
     16   EGA 16 colours
     48   VGA 16 colours
     64   EGA 64 colours
    128   EGA 128 colours
    192   VGA 64 colours
    384   VGA 128 colours

If either of the two high bits of any byte is set then it is not valid. (It
might be used in future if necessary to make a DER format palette file, in
case the existing functions are insufficient.)

In the current implementation, more than 16 colours is not meaningful.

The format for EGA is each colour has one byte, and the high two bits must
be clear. The bits are made "00rgbRGB" where the 6-bit output value will
be bits made "RrRrRr" red, "GgGgGg" green, "BbBbBb" blue.

The format for VGA is each colour has three bytes, in order red, green,
blue, each value being in range zero to sixty-three.


=== *.SCR ===

Screen definitions.

The header is (each coordinate, etc are one byte each):

* Either 0x00 or 0x80; if 0x80 then it has a variable property list, which
comes immediately after the header. See the .BRD format specification for
details about the format of this variable property list.

* Flags.

* The border colour.

* Character codes for the four default borders (E, N, W, S); zero means no
default character code for the border.

* Coordinates of the four soft edges (only one byte per edge). These are
the inner edges beyond which it may scroll if the cursor or player moves
beyond these edges, unless doing so would violate the hard edges.

* Coordinates of the four hard edges (only one byte per edge). These are
the inner edges in which the edge of the board or text window must fit; the
outside of the board cannot be beyond them unless the board is too small.

* X and Y coordinates of centre of viewport.

* X and Y coordinates of message text or popup text window title. If the Y
coordinate is 255 then the message text is not displayed.

* Left and right edges of message text.

The flags are:

* bit0 = Set to left align message text, or clear for centre. Left
alignment will be at the X coordinate if possible, moving to the left up
to the left message edge if it is needed in order to fit. Centre alignment
is centred at the specified message X coordinate.

* bit1 = If set, then message text is flashy like in ZZT; if clear, then it
will instead use the colour defined in the screen where the text is written
(which may be multiple colours if they are not all the same in all of the
places where the characters of the message text are written). This is not
meaningful for text windows.

* bit2 = If set, then the EXIT indicators will display the border character
instead of blank.

* bit3 = If set, then the USER indicators will display the border character
instead of blank.

* bit4 = Disable scrolling on this screen if set.

* bit5 = If set, then message text has character zero added on the left and
right edge of the text. If clear, then this extra edge is omitted.

The screen data is compressed with the following commands:

* 0-79 = Copy previous cell 1 to 80 times.

* 80-159 = Copy above cells (i.e. 80 behind) 1 to 80 times.

* 160-239 = Follow by the command byte and colour byte, and then 1 to 80
more bytes being the parameters for that many cells.

* 240-255 = A single cell. If bit0 is set then the command byte follows;
if bit1 is set then the colour byte follows; if bit2 is set then the
parameter byte follows. Any omitted bytes are copied from the previous
cell if bit3 is clear, or are zero if bit3 is set.

The commands are:

* SC_BACKGROUND = Display a fixed colour and character (parameter). If bit0
is set then uses the character, if bit1 is set then uses the colour; clear
bits mean to display what was previously displayed on the screen instead.

* SC_BOARD = Display the contents of the board. If it is out of range, then
it displays the fixed colour and character (like SC_BACKGROUND) if bit0 is
set, except that if the border character is nonzero and it is exactly one
step beyond the edge of the board, and bit1 is set, then it will display
the border colour and character instead. If bit2 of the kind is set then
the overlay is suppressed. Clearing bit0 will cause it to display the tile
of the nearest edge instead.

* SC_NUMERIC = Display the value of a status variable. The low nybble of
the kind specifies which variable. The high nybble of the parameter means
which numeric format to use (defined in the NUMFORM lump), and the low
nybble of the parameter means the digit position (0 for the rightmost).

* SC_NUMERIC_SPECIAL = Same as SC_NUMERIC but one of the special values
below instead of a general-purpose status variable.

* SC_MEMORY = The colour is the high byte and the parameter is the low byte
of a memory address; the low byte of the value at that address is the
character to display, and the high byte is the colour to display.

* SC_INDICATOR = One of the indicators. The parameter means the character
to display if the indicator is set; most indicators will be a blank space
if the indicator is clear but there are some exceptions. See below for the
list of valid indicators.

* SC_TEXT = Text in the text window. The left hard edge is the left edge of
the text, and the viewport X is used for centred text.

* SC_ITEM = (Reserved for future use.)

* SC_BITS_0_LO = The low 16-bits of status variable 0; shows the parameter
character if set, or a blank space if clear. The low nybble of the kind
means which bit to use.

* SC_BITS_0_HI = Same as SC_BITS_0_LO but the high 16-bits instead.

* SC_BITS_1_LO = These and more, up to SC_BITS_3_HI, are like the above
two kinds but for status variables numbered 1 to 3 instead.

Special values for SC_NUMERIC_SPECIAL:

* SC_SPEC_PLAYER_X = X coordinate of first XY record of stat 1.

* SC_SPEC_PLAYER_Y = Y coordinate of first XY record of stat 1.

* SC_SPEC_CAMERA_X = X coordinate of centre of viewport.

* SC_SPEC_CAMERA_Y = Y coordinate of centre of viewport.

* SC_SPEC_TEXT_SCROLL_PERCENT = If this is a text window, a number from 0
to 100 indicating how far down scrolled it is from the top.

* SC_SPEC_TEXT_LINE_NUMBER = If this is a text window, the 1-based line
number of the cursor.

* SC_SPEC_TEXT_LINE_COUNT = If this is a text window, the total number of
lines of text.

* SC_SPEC_CURRENT_BOARD = The current board number.

* SC_SPEC_EXIT_E = The east exit of the current board.

* SC_SPEC_EXIT_N = The north exit of the current board.

* SC_SPEC_EXIT_W = The west exit of the current board.

* SC_SPEC_EXIT_S = The south exit of the current board.

* SC_SPEC_WIDTH = The width of the current board.

* SC_SPEC_HEIGHT = The height of the current board.

* SC_SPEC_USERDATA = The user data field of the current board.

Indicators for SC_INDICATOR:

* SC_IND_CURSOR = The cursor position or player position. Displayed if
either the X or Y coordinate is aligned with the indicator.

* SC_IND_SCROLL = Displayed if it is possible to scroll in the direction
where this is relative to the nearest hard edge.

* SC_IND_EXIT_E = Displayed if the east exit is nonzero.

* SC_IND_EXIT_N = Displayed if the north exit is nonzero.

* SC_IND_EXIT_W = Displayed if the west exit is nonzero.

* SC_IND_EXIT_S = Displayed if the south exit is nonzero.

* SC_IND_USER0 = Displayed if the user0 flag of the board is set.

* SC_IND_USER1 = Displayed if the user1 flag of the board is set.

* SC_IND_USER2 = Displayed if the user2 flag of the board is set.

* SC_IND_USER3 = Displayed if the user3 flag of the board is set.


=== *.SND ===

Wave sounds. The format is: the header, and then the audio data.

* Number of header bytes (8-bits; does not count this one). This must be
4 or else it is an error; later versions might add more, or use a different
format in which case this number will not match the number of header bytes
of any valid formats.

* First flags (8-bits).

* Codec number (low nybble) and second flags (high nybble).

* Sample rate (16-bits). (See the flags below)

The codec numbers are:
  0 = Unsigned 8-bits
  1 = Creative 4-bits to 8-bits ADPCM
  2 = Creative 3-bits to 8-bits ADPCM
  3 = Creative 2-bits to 8-bits ADPCM
  4 = Signed 16-bits
  5 = (unused)
  6 = A-law
  7 = mu-law

The flags are:

* bit0 = Specifies the working of the sample rate, where 1 means hertz and
0 means the Creative Voice format.

* bit1 = Set if the filter should be enabled.

* bit2 = Set for stereo. (Super ZZ Zero does not currently support stereo.)


=== *.WIN ===

Window info; this is optionally present for each screen, and is only used
if the screen is used for a text window or item window.

This starts with a header. The first byte is a flag byte, and the second
byte is currently unused and must be zero.

The flags are:

* bit0 = Set if the text window has single ends, clear if the ends are
extended to the entire size of the window.

* bit1 = Set for zero-based line numbers.

After that is the commands. If the high bit is set, then:

* bit4-bit0 = Command code

* bit5 = Set if new parameter follows

* bit6 = Set if new colour follows

If the high bit is clear, then it specifies how many cells will copy the
previous cell. Values higher than eighty are reserved.

See help/editwin.hlp for an explanation of the command codes.

The parameter is usually a character code, but zero can be used to mean
to use the character within the screen at that position instead. In some
cases the parameter can be 255 which also has a special meaning.

The colour can be 0x11 for the primary colour or 0x22 for the secondary
colour; other numbers will use that number as the colour.


=== BRD.NAM ===

This lump starts with the 16-bit highest board number, and then it is
followed by null-terminated strings being the board names (starting with
board zero). (Note that it is possible for a board to have a name even
though there is no such board, or to lack a name even if it exists.)


=== CATALOG.DER ===

This lump contains metadata which is not used by Super ZZ Zero itself,
but may be used by external programs such as catalog services. This lump
is present if and only if the !SZ0 lump specifies that the file is a
published world file. If the world file is modified, this lump will
automatically be deleted and it will be marked as unpublished.

This lump (and the associated "side file") is documented separately.


=== CURRENT.BRD ===

Same as the *.BRD format; used only in save game files, which contains the
current state of the current board.

However, if bit15 of the encoding flags is set (which is only the case for
the CURRENT.BRD lump), then additional data follows after the stats, which
is stat index numbers stored in the board grid. They are in order by the
under layer, main layer, over layer. There is one byte per tile, but if it
is zero then a 16-bit number of how many more zeros follow.


=== DYNASTR ===

This lump is only allowed in a save game file (but it is not mandatory); it
is an error if it is present in a world file.

This lump contains dynamic strings. The first byte is the number of dynamic
strings, and then each dynamic string (1-based numbering) consists of one
byte specifying the length of the text, and then the text.


=== ELEMENT ===

This starts with the 128 bytes for the appearance mapping.

After that will be four animation specifications, which are:

* Mode (1 byte).

* Steps (4 bytes): They will be the offset of appearance mapping, adding to
the offsets specified by the elements.

After that is element definitions. Each element definition consists of:

* Flag byte. The low nybble is the number of bytes of the name length; if
it is zero, then the high nybble is how many definitions to be skipped (in
addition to the current one).

* The name of this element.

* If flag bit7 is set, the major appearance code (one byte).

* If flag bit6 is set, the minor appearance code (one byte).

* If flag bit5 is set, the attributes (four bytes); otherwise it will have
the same attributes as the previous definition.

* If flag bit4 is set, then it has events; there is a 16-bit number with
a bit which is set means an event is present, and then it is followed by
the 16-bit event addresses for each event from 0 to 15.

If neither flag bit7 nor bit6 is set, then the major appearance code will
be AP_PARAM and the minor code will be zero. Otherwise, whichever code is
not set will be zero (if it is the major code, zero is AP_FIXED).


=== GENERAL.DER ===

This lump is optional, and is only needed if any of the features specified
by this lump are used. This contains a ASN.1X sequence with the fields:

* A set of object identifiers specifying features which are mandatory. If
this set contains an unrecognized object identifier, then it is an error.
Any item may also be a sequence of object identifiers; in this case, any
one of them is suitable, where the first one in each sequence is the first
preference over the other ones in case multiple of them are.

* A set of object identifiers specifying features which are used by this
world file but are optional. It is not an error if it contains an
unrecognized object identifier, but it may warn the user about it.

OIDs which are expected in the mandatory and optional sets are:

* ...0.(major).(minor).(patch) for a version number; the (patch) is
optional and should normally only be used in the optional set. The
version number without the (patch) should be used in the mandatory set.
If in future it becomes necessary to emulate bugs of older versions of
Super ZZ Zero, it will use this version number to enable such emulation.

* ...4.0.(height) means that the world uses custom fonts and/or palettes.
The (height) must be fourteen; other numbers are not currently supported.

All of the further fields are optional. Each one is a implicit typed
sequence with context-specific class; the type number indicates the
meaning of the sequence, and they are:

* 0 = Joystick mapping. There can be up to 32 mappings defined; each is
a sequence, where the first item in the sequence is a PC string with the
button names with semicolons in between, and the second item is a octet
string, with a format described below.

* 1 = Default font/palette. It contains two Visible strings, the first
being the font name and the second being the palette name, in both cases
without the suffix (.CHR, .FNT, or .PAL). Either one may be null to use
the built-in font/palette.

The octet strings in the joystick mappings are made as a sequence of the
mappings for levels 0 to 15; there can be less than sixteen mappings in
which case the rest are null. Each byte will be one of:

* 0 to 3 means 1 to 4 null entries.

* 4 to 7 means 2 to 5 copies of the previous non-null entry.

* 8, 9, 13, 16, 17, 24 to 27, and 30 to 126 means a ASCII key code.

* 10 to 12 means the next 1 to 3 bytes are command codes. (See config.doc
for a list of the possible command codes.)

* 128 to 135 means shift while held, to level 1 to 8.

* Adding 128 to a ASCII key code will mean auto fire.


=== GLOBAL ===

An uncompressed global script, which runs on all boards that it is not
suppressed by. This is optional.


=== MEMORY ===

This lump is mandatory.

The initial state of the program memory (not compressed). If it is too
small then the rest will be zero.


=== MEMORY.ED ===

Like MEMORY but optional and only used in the editor, and there is some
compression applied. For this lump, the starting address is 0x100 instead
of zero.

Each command of the compression code has the high 2-bits specifying the
command code and the low 6-bits being one less than the run length. The
possible command codes are:

* 0 = Homogeneous; all zero

* 1 = Heterogeneous; 8-bit data

* 2 = Heterogeneous; 16-bit data

* 3 = (Reserved)


=== NUMFORM ===

The numeric formats. There are sixteen records, each of which contains:

* The format type (1 byte): One of the NF_ constants in common.h.

* The leading character (1 byte): Used mainly for leading zeros, or for
blank space in some other format types.

* The mark character (1 byte): Not used for all format types. Some types
use it for the nonblank character.

* The division amount (1 byte): Amount to divide the value by before being
converted into the numeric format. (This is mostly useful with meters, but
can be used with any format type.) (This division rounds down for most
formats (although in some cases, it does not round to an integer), but
for meters it will round up.)

If this lump is not present, then only formats 0 and 1 are defined, both of
which are decimal numeric formats, where format 0 means leading spaces and
format 1 means leading zeros.

Format types:

* NF_DECIMAL 'd' = Decimal.

* NF_HEX_UPPER 'X' = Uppercase hexadecimal.

* NF_HEX_LOWER 'x' = Lowercase hexadecimal.

* NF_OCTAL 'o' = Octal.

* NF_COMMA ',' = Display the mark character if this digit position would be
nonblank if it was using a decimal format.

* NF_ROMAN 'R' = Roman numerals. The mark character is the code of the
letter 'A'; you can switch this to 'a' for lowercase. In this case, it is
left-aligned and digit position 0 is the leftmost letter. If the division
amount is 2 then it will display the letter 'S' to mean half. The leading
character is used for all positions if the value is zero.

* NF_LSD_MONEY 'L' = Display currency amount by predecimal English money.
Digit position 0 is for farthings, which is blank unless the division
amount is 2 or 4 in which case it is the number of farthings (from 0 to 3).
Positions 1 and 2 are for pence, 3 and 4 for shillings, and 5 and the rest
are for pounds.

* NF_METER 'm' = Display the mark character if the value is greater than
the digit position, or the leading character otherwise.

* NF_METER_HALF 'h' = Display character code 219 if the value is greater
than twice the digit position, the leading character if less or equal, or
the mark character if it is exactly one less than twice the position.

* NF_METER_EXT 'M' = Like NF_METER but for additional positions.

* NF_METER_HALF_EXT 'H' = Like NF_METER_HALF but for additional positions.

* NF_BINARY 'b' = Display the mark character if a bit is set or the leading
character if a bit is clear.

* NF_BINARY_EXT 'B' = Like NF_BINARY but for additional positions.

* NF_CHARACTER 'c' = The display will be the character whose code is the
low 8-bits of the value. If the digit position is nonzero then it will
correspond to the next 8-bits, etc.

* NF_NONZERO '!' = Display the mark character if the value is nonzero or
the leading character if the value is zero (or if it is less than one after
the division is made).

* NF_BOARD_NAME 'n' = The first half of the board name.

* NF_BOARD_NAME_EXT 'N' = The last half of the board name.


=== PACK ===

Not currently used. In future, might be used for storing the .asm file and
some editor-only lumps in a compressed pack, for distributing the game when
you want to include the editor-related stuff; it is omitted if you are not
including editor-related stuff. An external program is used to unpack it.


=== PREVIOUS.BRD ===

This has the same format as CURRENT.BRD, and is used when the control flags
to save/restore the board are used.


=== SAVE ===

This lump is only for save game files, but it is no longer used in the
current version; see SAVE.DER instead.


=== SAVE.DER ===

Only used in save game files. See savegame.c for the definition.


=== SCR.NAM ===

Same format as BRD.NAM but for screens instead of boards. (This is only
used in the editor.)


=== START ===

This lump is mandatory in world files; it is omitted in save game files.

It contains:

* Flags (16-bits).

* Starting board number (16-bits).

* A 16-bit number, where any bit which is set means that the status
variable is initialized to zero and is not included in this lump; if the
bit is clear then the value is specified explicitly (see below).

* Six reserved bytes which must be set to zero.

* Status variables (sixteen, of 32-bits each). One of the above fields has
bits to specify if any of these are omitted.

The flags are:

* bit0 = Clear to automatically pause the game when it starts.

* bit1 = Forty columns mode.


=== TEMPLATE.DER ===

This is only used in templates. It contains a sequence of operations to be
applied to the world file when being created from a template (and then this
lump is deleted from the newly created world file). Each operation is a
implicit typed sequence.

* 0 = Contains Visible strings, each of which is the name of a lump to be
deleted from the new world file.


=== TEXT ===

Contains null-terminated global text strings, starting with string 1 (with
no other data). (String 0 is always blank.)


=== TEXT.ED ===

Like TEXT but only used in the editor.

